# Simplified Chinese translation for luci-app-fm160.
#
# This file is the source of truth for the translation; it is compiled
# into fm160.zh-cn.lmo at package build time.  Two things about its
# shape are deliberate and are checked by tools/i18n/check.py:
#
#   The daemon labels.  fm160d publishes its dial step names and its
#   profile verdicts as strings, and the front end renders them through
#   _() at the point of display.  No extractor can see those, because
#   they only exist at run time, so they are hand-written msgids here
#   and the checker derives the required set from the C source.  Free-
#   form diagnostic text (dial.last_error) is NOT translated: it names
#   a specific command, profile number or AT error code, and a half-
#   translated error is worse than an English one.
#
#   The coverage.  This file carries every string the front end can
#   show: all nine javascript files, the menu titles, and the labels
#   fm160d publishes from net.c and usbmode.c.  tools/i18n/check.py
#   fails on any message it can extract that is missing here, so a new
#   string added to a page is a red gate at the time it is written
#   rather than an English line that stays English.
#
#   The strings that are their own translation (an AT command, an
#   acronym the UI means literally) are present with an identical
#   msgstr on purpose: po2lmo omits them from the .lmo, which is what
#   the readers would fall back to anyway, and carrying them keeps the
#   rule above simple.
#
#   The msgctxt entries.  luci-base ships its own zh-cn catalogue and
#   load_catalog() merges EVERY *.zh-cn.lmo in the directory into one
#   namespace, so a msgid we share with luci-base is stored twice and
#   resolved by whichever archive the reader reaches first -- and it is
#   not the same archive for the server and the browser, because
#   lmo_translate_ctxt() returns the FIRST match while window.TR is
#   built by lmo_iterate() with last-write-wins.  Where our translation
#   differs from luci-base's, that is a visible coin toss, so those
#   messages carry a context and occupy a key luci-base never writes.
#   Sixteen do so today, and the list is whatever the checker finds
#   rather than a number maintained here -- translating the rest of
#   the pages is what turned four of them into sixteen, because most
#   of the collisions are ordinary LuCI words (Band, Speed, Down, Up,
#   Diagnostics, To) that only collide once they are translated at
#   all.  The shared msgids that remain translate identically to
#   luci-base's, so the outcome does not depend on which archive wins
#   and they are left shared on purpose -- keeping them means following
#   any later fix to luci-base's wording.  The checker requires both
#   halves of that: a differing translation must carry a context, and a
#   context must not appear where nothing collides.  A menu title cannot
#   take a context (the menu tree carries only `title`), which is why the
#   Signal page is called "Signal Quality".
